1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,380
A main battle tank weighs
more than sixty tonnes.

2
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:08,020
And here it is, crossing a river on a
bridge made of floating metal boxes.

3
00:00:08,781 --> 00:00:11,541
No pillars. No foundations
in the riverbed.

4
00:00:11,801 --> 00:00:16,081
Just sections resting on the water,
locked together, with a road on top.

5
00:00:16,686 --> 00:00:19,686
It looks like something that
should work perfectly well...

6
00:00:19,786 --> 00:00:21,686
right up until the tank arrives.

7
00:00:22,478 --> 00:00:25,038
So how does floating
metal carry sixty tonnes?

8
00:00:26,115 --> 00:00:28,235
The tank adds sixty tonnes of weight.

9
00:00:28,315 --> 00:00:32,335
So the bridge must displace
sixty tonnes of extra water.

10
00:00:32,997 --> 00:00:35,397
In a river, that's sixty cubic metres.

11
00:00:35,957 --> 00:00:40,537
A block of water ten metres long,
six metres wide, and one metre deep.

12
00:00:40,759 --> 00:00:44,019
That sounds like a lot.
But a bridge is long.

13
00:00:44,276 --> 00:00:46,976
And that block isn't
pushed aside by one box.

14
00:00:47,756 --> 00:00:50,896
It's shared between all the
sections the tank's weight reaches.

15
00:00:51,585 --> 00:00:55,465
The tank drives on. The
pontoons settle lower.

16
00:00:55,885 --> 00:00:59,165
They push aside more water, and
the water pushes back harder.

17
00:00:59,791 --> 00:01:01,611
No pumps. No computer.

18
00:01:02,251 --> 00:01:05,051
The bridge finds its new
balance all on its own.

19
00:01:05,628 --> 00:01:08,728
It's the same rule that keeps
a steel ship afloat, and

20
00:01:08,958 --> 00:01:12,208
Archimedes wrote it down more
than two thousand years ago.

21
00:01:12,743 --> 00:01:15,643
Near Seattle, a motorway
crosses Lake Washington on

22
00:01:15,873 --> 00:01:21,803
seventy-seven concrete pontoons, more
than two kilometres long. Same physics.

23
00:01:22,543 --> 00:01:25,403
But this bridge is designed
to last for decades.

24
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:28,940
So floating is the easy part.

25
00:01:29,659 --> 00:01:31,299
Here's the hard part.

26
00:01:32,019 --> 00:01:34,899
The British Army says eight of
its amphibious bridging vehicles

27
00:01:35,129 --> 00:01:38,899
can build a bridge a hundred metres
long in about thirty minutes.

28
00:01:39,591 --> 00:01:42,371
If an army can build a
bridge in thirty minutes...

29
00:01:42,471 --> 00:01:45,091
why can't the enemy simply destroy it?

30
00:01:45,418 --> 00:01:48,898
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

31
00:01:49,518 --> 00:01:51,118
It really helps the channel.

32
00:01:53,508 --> 00:01:54,708
We'll get there.

33
00:01:54,928 --> 00:01:59,028
But before the enemy gets a say, the
bridge has to survive the tank itself.

34
00:01:59,793 --> 00:02:03,693
Put a heavy truck on a narrow boat,
and the boat may float just fine.

35
00:02:04,433 --> 00:02:06,173
It just floats on its side.

36
00:02:06,803 --> 00:02:09,143
A bridge section is wide and flat.

37
00:02:09,903 --> 00:02:12,303
When one edge dips, that
edge pushes aside more

38
00:02:12,533 --> 00:02:15,443
water, and the extra
lift pushes it back up.

39
00:02:15,589 --> 00:02:17,469
And the sections are locked together.

40
00:02:18,049 --> 00:02:20,269
The tank isn't standing on one box.

41
00:02:20,869 --> 00:02:24,569
It's standing on a long strip that
shares its weight along the bridge.

42
00:02:25,049 --> 00:02:28,369
That's why one common design
is called a ribbon bridge.

43
00:02:28,667 --> 00:02:29,847
But a ribbon bends.

44
00:02:29,927 --> 00:02:35,207
The joints flex, the deck dips under every
axle, and the whole thing rides the waves.

45
00:02:35,907 --> 00:02:38,407
Engineers don't try to stop that.

46
00:02:38,807 --> 00:02:40,587
They keep the movement within limits.

47
00:02:40,934 --> 00:02:44,914
And here's a catch. A tank and
a truck can weigh exactly the

48
00:02:45,144 --> 00:02:47,774
same, and still not be
equally safe to cross.

49
00:02:48,477 --> 00:02:51,337
A tank spreads its weight
along two long tracks.

50
00:02:52,117 --> 00:02:54,577
A truck puts all of it onto a few axles.

51
00:02:55,167 --> 00:02:59,227
Military bridges also use a system
called Military Load Classification.

52
00:02:59,457 --> 00:03:04,767
Because a vehicle's weight alone
doesn't tell you how it loads a bridge.

53
00:03:05,356 --> 00:03:06,716
And then there's the river.

54
00:03:07,536 --> 00:03:10,556
The current pushes on every pontoon,
so the bridge has to be held

55
00:03:10,786 --> 00:03:14,916
in place, with anchors, cables,
or small boats pushing upstream.

56
00:03:15,801 --> 00:03:18,661
A floating bridge can have
more than enough buoyancy...

57
00:03:18,881 --> 00:03:21,741
and still fail, because the
river pushed it sideways.

58
00:03:22,838 --> 00:03:24,758
Which brings us back to the question.

59
00:03:25,478 --> 00:03:28,098
Why would an army rely on
something this delicate?

60
00:03:30,628 --> 00:03:34,048
Because a river stops an army
better than almost anything else.

61
00:03:34,388 --> 00:03:38,728
And a retreating defender will
often blow up the bridges behind it.

62
00:03:38,895 --> 00:03:41,055
So the attacker brings their own.

63
00:03:41,679 --> 00:03:44,159
Some bridges travel folded on trucks.

64
00:03:44,979 --> 00:03:48,499
Each section slides into the
water and unfolds on its own.

65
00:03:49,052 --> 00:03:51,292
Others are vehicles
that become the bridge.

66
00:03:51,732 --> 00:03:56,192
They drive into the river, swing out
their floats, and lock onto the next one.

67
00:03:56,723 --> 00:04:00,803
In 2016, British and German crews
linked thirty of them into a bridge

68
00:04:01,033 --> 00:04:05,283
three hundred and fifty metres
long across the Vistula, in Poland.

69
00:04:06,014 --> 00:04:08,134
But thirty minutes is the assembly.

70
00:04:08,274 --> 00:04:09,834
Not the operation.

71
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:12,840
The equipment has to reach the river.

72
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:15,420
The banks need a way on and off.

73
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:19,160
And everything that wants to
cross has to wait its turn.

74
00:04:19,759 --> 00:04:21,859
That waiting is the real weakness.

75
00:04:24,619 --> 00:04:29,159
In May 2022, Russian forces tried
to cross the Siverskyi Donets

76
00:04:29,239 --> 00:04:33,239
river near the village of
Bilohorivka, in eastern Ukraine.

77
00:04:33,931 --> 00:04:36,531
The crossing site was not a surprise.

78
00:04:36,711 --> 00:04:40,451
Ukrainian commanders had
expected an attempt in this area.

79
00:04:41,111 --> 00:04:45,791
Russian forces tried to hide their
preparations with smoke and burning trees.

80
00:04:46,471 --> 00:04:49,931
But Ukrainian reconnaissance
confirmed the bridge was going up.

81
00:04:50,447 --> 00:04:51,607
Artillery opened fire.

82
00:04:51,837 --> 00:04:53,747
Airstrikes followed.

83
00:04:54,448 --> 00:04:56,848
Images later showed
three destroyed pontoon

84
00:04:57,078 --> 00:05:00,288
bridges and dozens of
wrecked military vehicles.

85
00:05:01,072 --> 00:05:04,692
British defence intelligence assessed
that Russia had lost significant

86
00:05:04,922 --> 00:05:09,272
armoured manoeuvre elements of at
least one battalion tactical group.

87
00:05:11,102 --> 00:05:13,682
Notice what failed. Not the buoyancy.

88
00:05:14,322 --> 00:05:15,962
The pontoons could float.

89
00:05:16,322 --> 00:05:18,682
But that wasn't enough to
make the crossing succeed.

90
00:05:19,565 --> 00:05:21,305
The crossing couldn't be protected.

91
00:05:21,385 --> 00:05:25,465
And a bridge tells the enemy
exactly where you're going to be.

92
00:05:25,685 --> 00:05:28,265
Every vehicle has to pass
through one narrow point...

93
00:05:28,495 --> 00:05:30,845
and wait its turn to do it.

94
00:05:31,398 --> 00:05:33,818
A pontoon bridge isn't just a road.

95
00:05:34,418 --> 00:05:36,598
It's a target with a queue in front of it.

96
00:05:39,028 --> 00:05:41,308
So yes, the enemy can destroy it.

97
00:05:41,388 --> 00:05:42,988
And often does.

98
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:47,410
But destroying a bridge once isn't
the same as stopping the crossing.

99
00:05:48,118 --> 00:05:51,038
A damaged section can
sometimes be swapped out.

100
00:05:51,458 --> 00:05:55,658
Engineers can repair the crossing,
or build a new one somewhere else.

101
00:05:56,398 --> 00:05:59,138
In some systems, the same
modules can be reconfigured

102
00:05:59,368 --> 00:06:02,898
as rafts, ferrying vehicles
across one at a time.

103
00:06:03,150 --> 00:06:05,050
So the crossing becomes a race.

104
00:06:05,850 --> 00:06:09,770
One side tries to cut the flow of
fuel, ammunition and reinforcements.

105
00:06:10,670 --> 00:06:12,670
The other tries to restore it faster.

106
00:06:13,490 --> 00:06:17,690
Engineers may use darkness and smoke,
or set up alternative crossing sites.

107
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:21,030
But none of that guarantees safety.

108
00:06:21,759 --> 00:06:23,779
Speed is one of its few protections.

109
00:06:23,919 --> 00:06:26,939
But only until the enemy finds it.

110
00:06:29,219 --> 00:06:31,539
So floating was never the hard part.

111
00:06:31,719 --> 00:06:35,119
The hard part is staying
alive long enough to matter.

112
00:06:35,381 --> 00:06:37,381
And that race keeps getting faster.

113
00:06:38,381 --> 00:06:40,901
If the enemy can find a
new bridge within minutes,

114
00:06:40,981 --> 00:06:43,561
half an hour to build
one starts to look slow.

115
00:06:44,247 --> 00:06:46,507
The bridge floats. The tank rolls.

116
00:06:46,587 --> 00:06:49,557
And for a brief moment,
a river becomes a road.
